  "knowledgePattern": "Convert cyclic variable-base logarithms to one common base",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "algebra"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>. These are all nonzero because none of x,y,z equals 1. The definitions give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p>The first equality becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Similarly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Multiplying the three equalities gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></math>. This is exactly the requested product. Answer A.</p><p>The conditions are attainable, for example x = y = z = 8. The nonzero logarithms justify every division and cancellation used.</p>",
